# philosophy: pavement saves nature 🦉🗿🌱

> **pavement saves nature.**

one claim, true at two scales: the **why** under every externalization rule the learner carries, and under the rule to *read* what is already there.

## the bridge — where the metaphor turns literal

**pavement is reusable prethought** — a brief, a howto, a term cluster, a resolved dispute, a paved route. somebody already spent the energy, and wrote the result where the next traveler picks it up for the cost of a read.

```
reuse the pavement → do not re-derive → do not re-spend the energy → save nature
```

## the asymmetry — this is what makes it compound

| | cost | paid by |
|---|---|---|
| **pave it** | once | the first traveler |
| **improvise it** | every time | every traveler, forever |

⇒ so *"write more briefs"* is **not** the lesson. it is *"write the ones that will be walked, and then walk them."*

## 🟡 the two failures

| failure | shape | correction |
|---|---|---|
| **the braided trail** | a second path laid beside a serviceable one, because the author did not look | check the pavement before you lay more |
| **the rut** | conformity to a found path, with no test of whether it leads anywhere | check that the pavement is not a rut |

⇒ **precedent is a reason to look, never a reason to conform.**

⇒ the repair for both: **do not widen the braid — mend the tread.** if a paved path is genuinely bad, **fix the pavement**; do not walk beside it.

a traveler who must think **should think**; offroad exploration is honest work. the philosophy asks one move first: **check for a path** — and when none exists, walk it and **leave a route behind**.

> pave it once, so it is never paid for twice.
> then **walk it** — an unwalked path is the only wasted one. 🦉🗿🌱
